# terra-high-a benchmark report

Run start: 2026-11-13 16:44:42 CST (22:44:42 UTC). End: 2026-11-13 17:25:39 CST (23:25:39 UTC); elapsed 40 min 57 s. All clock times below are CST. This session identifies itself as Codex, GPT-6; the exact deployed model ID and reasoning effort are not exposed to this assistant. The run name suggests terra/high but that is not independent confirmation.

All work is in this worktree. The models are in `cad/_wip/sw-eval/terra-high-a/`; scripts, logs, nodal arrays, pictures and `results.json` are in this report's folder. No install, shared settings change, commit, or push. I used a separately owned hidden SolidWorks instance for every `job(wait_s=1800)`; I disabled `swkit.pin_export_prefs` in my scripts because `job()` otherwise writes machine-wide export preferences, contrary to the explicit instruction. Exports were checked under the existing settings. All meshes that solved were below 200,000 nodes.
45:Same bore load and hole support, 3.0/2.2/1.7 mm: 23,409/43,671/86,565 nodes; reactions −150 N X and displacements 2.622/2.639/2.646 mm. VM picked at the geometric root line was **10.759/9.594/13.599 MPa**; away-mask VM was 14.384/15.127/16.250 MPa and Z shear 5.608/4.981/7.174 MPa. The root line is mesh-dependent, and its final changes fail 5%; no finite converged sharp-corner peak or pass/fail verdict is established. The 4 mm fillet study is the physically rounded comparison, but it also needs the other checks listed under T3. Do not treat 13.599 MPa as a converged design stress.
76:The smallest margin is the **2.02 shear factor**: 20% more load would reduce it to **1.68**, and an actual print may have a lower shear allowable. A 3 mm rather than 2 mm rear pad would add another approximately 1.65% of the original mass, remaining under 10%; a simple inverse-thickness estimate reduces this shear from 6.45 to about 5.73 MPa (factor 2.27), but that option was not modeled and the estimate is not a verdict. Check real rear clearances, layer properties, fastener preload, fatigue, the shallow-patch idealization, print support, and physical testing before use.
80:The nominal projected bearing pressure is 500/(10.2·30) = **1.634 MPa**; that is an average pressure, not a prediction of the peak von Mises stress. Model: Ø10 steel pin through Ø10.2 PETG block hole; 0.1 mm radial gap. Block bottom fixed. Each pin end carries +250 N Z. No-penetration contact uses Intel direct sparse; separate bonded soft pads on the pin ends have radius 2.5 mm, length 2 mm and E=0.5 MPa, with outer faces fixed. `t6c_contact.png` shows the geometry and pads. Under the finest full load, the bound estimated from maximum pad traction times both pad areas is 1.77 N, 0.35% of 500 N. This is an idealized static no-penetration contact model (friction was not independently characterized): no manufacturing error, preload, wear, friction, or fatigue.
112:- T4 still needs a defensible finite sharp-corner judgment and support/load sensitivity; it remains **not shown**. T5 is a narrowly passing linear-static result, not a print or assembly authorization; the rear pad fit and actual fasteners have not been checked.
114:T6 model SHA-256: `b2eaa9eed3d51cc135eb0943881895551e8e18e86f106996f560c0b617fc99d7`. The image outputs were opened after each sheet run; the visual observations are reflected in the task sections above. T2 original feature IDs were not compared before and after, although the original named features remain in the saved tree.
